          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You already used hables for one friend. Today add only the other two singular slots:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "present subjunctive"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. present subjunctive: hable.",
                "person: tú. present subjunctive: hables.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. present subjunctive: hable."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural forms wait. Say the small row: hable, hables, hable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the yo stem, then a vowel swap",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Start from present hablo. Remove -o to reveal habl-. Regular -ar verbs then use e in these subjunctive endings: -e, -es, -e. The family vowel has swapped from the familiar a to e. This recipe will matter later because a verb's special present yo stem can travel with it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The name, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish subjuntivo descends from Latin subiūnctīvus, “subjoined,” from sub- “under” plus iungere “join.” English join, junction, and conjunction belong to the same word family; yoke is a more distant cousin. The name helps you picture the wanted clause joined under quiero que. It is history, not a complete usage rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Build each answer only from the learned singular row."
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C18-subjuntivo-forms",
              "prompt": "Give the learned singular present-subjunctive forms of hablar: yo, tú, then él/ella/usted.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-AR-SUBJUNCTIVE-SINGULAR",
                "ES-LEX-HABLAR"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 9,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "hable, hables, hable",
                "hable hables hable",
                "hable / hables / hable"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right: hable, hables, hable.",
                "incorrect": "Use the e-family row: hable, hables, hable."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C18-subjuntivo-recipe",
              "prompt": "Which present form gives the stem for regular hable: hablo or habla?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-SUBJUNCTIVE-YO-STEM-VOWEL-SWAP"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 6,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "hablo"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right: remove -o from hablo to get habl-.",
                "incorrect": "Start from the present yo form hablo, then remove -o."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C18-subjuntivo-name",
              "prompt": "What joining idea sits inside the Latin source of subjuntivo?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-ETYMON-SUBJUNCTIVE-SUBIUNCTIVUS"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 7,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "subjoined or joined under",
                "subjoined",
                "joined under"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right: subiūnctīvus carries the idea 'subjoined.'",
                "incorrect": "Think of a clause joined under another: subjoined."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Give the three forms. (Hable, hables, hable.) Which present form supplies the stem? (Hablo.) What did the family vowel do? (a becomes e.) What does the name remember? (A clause subjoined, or joined under another.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
